From: =?UTF-8?q?Lu=C3=ADs=20Duarte?= <lduarte@cloudflare.com>
Date: Fri, 9 Oct 2026 17:36:08 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] scope: index bindings by name and bind `using` in bodies

Every identifier reference, declaration and escape check looked its name
up with a linear Vec<Binding> scan comparing JsStrings, walking each
scope up the chain. Minified bundles keep thousands of bindings in one
flat function scope, so the scan is quadratic there: ~15% of compiling
react-dom on 0.20 (and ~27% on 0.22, where JsString comparison got more
expensive).

Keep a map from name to position beside the Vec. Bindings are
append-only and a name gets one position, so a hit is the same binding
the scan's first match returned.

Function, global and eval declaration instantiation matched only let and
const among lexical declarations, so a `using` or `await using` at the
top of a function body, script or eval got no binding. Its initializer
then wrote into a slot no scope had, and the VM panicked with an index
out of bounds in PutLexicalValue. Unpatched 0.22.0 panics the same way.

Blocks and modules already bind `using` like `let`, because the
bytecompiler has no disposal yet. Bind it the same way at these sites.
